Cypherverse — Future Real Payment Integration
Purpose

This document describes how to replace the current TEST payment system with a real payment provider later.

The marketplace order architecture should remain in place.

The goal is to replace the test payment processing layer without rebuilding the marketplace.

Current System

The current marketplace uses:

Frontend
   ↓
POST /api/marketplace/orders
   ↓
Marketplace Order
   ↓
POST /api/marketplace/orders/:orderId/payment
   ↓
Test Payment
   ↓
Test Confirm / Fail / Cancel


The current system is intentionally designed so that payment processing is separated from order creation.

Future Real Payment System

When real payments are introduced, the intended flow becomes:

User
 ↓
Frontend
 ↓
Create Marketplace Order
 ↓
Server determines authoritative price
 ↓
Create Payment
 ↓
Real Payment Provider
 ↓
Customer completes checkout
 ↓
Payment Provider processes payment
 ↓
Provider Webhook
 ↓
Cypherverse Server
 ↓
Verify Payment
 ↓
Mark Order Paid
 ↓
Complete Parcel Purchase
 ↓
Grant Ownership / Entitlement

Important Principle

The browser must never be the final authority for payment success.

Do not rely on:

Frontend says payment succeeded


Instead:

Payment Provider
        ↓
Verified server-side notification
        ↓
Cypherverse backend
        ↓
Order marked paid


The payment provider webhook should be treated as the authoritative payment confirmation.

Step 1 — Choose Payment Provider

Select the real provider.

Examples could include:

Stripe
PayPal
Another provider appropriate for the business

The provider should support server-side payment confirmation/webhooks.

Do not modify the marketplace order architecture just because the provider changes.

Step 2 — Add Provider Configuration

Add server-side environment variables.

Example:

PAYMENTS_MODE=production

PAYMENT_PROVIDER=stripe

PAYMENT_PROVIDER_SECRET_KEY=...

PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=...


Never expose secret payment credentials to the frontend.

Do not put secret keys in:

React code
browser localStorage
public environment variables
Git
client-side configuration

Step 3 — Keep Marketplace Orders

Continue using:

POST /api/marketplace/orders


The frontend still sends:

{
  "parcelId": "..."
}


The backend continues determining:

User
Parcel
Listing
Seller
Price
Currency
Quantity

The client should still never determine the authoritative purchase price.

Step 4 — Create the Payment

Continue using:

POST /api/marketplace/orders/:orderId/payment


The backend should:

Load the authenticated user's order.
Verify the order belongs to the user.
Verify the order can still be paid.
Read the authoritative order amount.
Create a payment-provider checkout/payment object.
Store the provider payment/checkout ID.
Return only the information the frontend needs to continue checkout.

Conceptually:

MarketplaceOrder
      ↓
authoritative amount
      ↓
Real Payment Provider
      ↓
Provider Payment ID
      ↓
MarketplacePayment

Step 5 — Store Provider IDs

The payment database record should associate the Cypherverse payment with the provider payment.

Conceptually:

MarketplacePayment
------------------
id
orderId
provider
providerPaymentId
amount
currency
status
createdAt
updatedAt


The exact schema depends on the existing Prisma models.

The important relationship is:

Cypherverse Order
        ↓
Cypherverse Payment
        ↓
Provider Payment

Step 6 — Frontend Checkout

The frontend should:

Create Order
     ↓
Create Payment
     ↓
Receive checkout information
     ↓
Open provider checkout
     ↓
User completes payment


The frontend can display:

Payment screen
Processing state
Success state
Failure state
Cancellation state

But the frontend should not directly grant ownership.

Step 7 — Provider Webhook

Add a server endpoint specifically for the payment provider.

Example:

POST /api/payments/webhook


The webhook must:

Receive the provider event.
Verify the provider signature.
Reject invalid signatures.
Identify the provider payment.
Find the corresponding Cypherverse payment.
Find the corresponding marketplace order.
Verify the amount and currency.
Determine the payment status.
Update the payment/order.
Complete the purchase when appropriate.
Step 8 — Verify the Payment

Never trust fields supplied directly by the browser.

The server should verify:

Provider payment ID
Provider status
Amount
Currency
Order association
Payment environment


For example:

Expected order amount:
$19.99

Provider-confirmed amount:
$19.99

Result:
VALID


If the provider reports:

$1.99


while the Cypherverse order is:

$19.99


the purchase must not be completed.

Step 9 — Make Webhooks Idempotent

Payment providers may send the same webhook more than once.

Therefore:

Webhook #1
   ↓
Process payment
   ↓
Order becomes PAID

Webhook #2
   ↓
Same event
   ↓
Already processed
   ↓
Do nothing


The purchase must never:

Transfer ownership twice
Grant an entitlement twice
Create duplicate purchases
Create duplicate fulfillment records

The server must make payment completion idempotent.

Step 10 — Complete the Marketplace Purchase

Only after verified successful payment:

Payment verified
      ↓
Order marked PAID
      ↓
Parcel ownership transferred
      ↓
Entitlement granted
      ↓
Listing closed/deactivated


These operations should occur in a safe server-side transaction where possible.

